📌 What are Workflow Files and Where Are They Stored?
GitHub Actions workflows are defined using YAML files with .yml or .yaml extensions.
These files must be stored inside the .github/workflows/ directory at the root of a GitHub repository.
GitHub automatically detects workflow files in this directory and uses their configurations to execute automated tasks.

🛠️ Practical Example:
Directory Structure:
my-project/
 │
 ├── .github/ 
 │           └── workflows/ 
 │                         ├── ci.yml
 │                         ├── cd.yml
 │                         └── test.yml
 ├── src/ 
 ├── README.md 
 └── Dockerfile

Example: ci.yml
name: CI Pipeline
  on:
     push:
         branches:
             - main  
 jobs:
    build:
        runs-on: ubuntu-latest
        steps:
        - name: Checkout Repository
          uses: actions/checkout@v4

        - name: Display Message
          run: echo "CI Workflow is running!"

🔍 How it works:
.github/ – Stores GitHub-specific configuration files.
workflows/ – Contains GitHub Actions workflow definitions.
ci.yml – Defines the Continuous Integration workflow.
cd.yml – Can define a Continuous Deployment workflow.
test.yml – Can define automated testing workflows.

💡 Key Takeaway:
The .github/workflows/ directory is the standard location for GitHub Actions workflow files. Organizing workflows properly makes CI/CD automation easier to manage and maintain.
